  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 101 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nBy Saturday morning a southern stream 500 MB low/trough will be\ncentered somewhere near the California/Arizona border. It will \nthen drift slowly east northeastward toward the central U.S. \nover the weekend and into early next week. By Monday evening it \nshould be located somewhere roughly around Nebraska, but by \nthat time a second 500 MB cyclone will have come ashore over the\nnorthwestern CONUS. As these two systems interact with each \nother the details of their evolution become more uncertain, but \nby Saturday night the surface trough discussed in the SHORT TERM\nsection above should be pushed across the Dakotas and lie in an\norientation parallel to the steering flow, roughly from the \ncentral High Plains northeastward near the Iowa/Minnesota border\nand into the upper Great Lakes. Frontogenetical forcing \nassociated with this increasingly baroclinic zone, beneath \nmodest but broad forcing for ascent aloft, will support \nincreasing rain potential near the boundary as we progress \nthrough the weekend. Most of this potential will initially lie \nnorth and west of our area, but POPs are introduced for our \nnorthwestern counties beginning Saturday night and continuing \nthrough Sunday night. The boundary/swath of rain will be slow to\nprogress any farther into Iowa until the main system/s move \nthrough sometime between Monday and Tuesday. Given this overall \nscenario, unseasonably warm conditions in place late this week \nwill continue through Saturday, with the approaching \ntrough/frontal zone/rain swath remaining northwest of Iowa \nduring that time, then be shunted steadily southeastward Sunday \nand Monday, with daily highs likely more in the 50s and 60s by \nTuesday and Wednesday of next week.",
